From d495dccb3fea492c44181f588420f4db69f3972c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 13:41:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] fix(update-pause): unsupported-scope notifier wakes
 and lease-wait steers still re-queue under the hold

Follow-up to ea4f9dc74e5 (PR #2168), the three non-blocking points of review 5890674054.

1. Turn-start gate for unsupported-scope wakes. The gate (claim_held) was installed only for a scope
   `supported()` accepts. A notifier wake it refuses (a detached route's local_automation_v1 Kanban wake) could
   still start inside the hold window. That turn is not exempt from the drain. `active_work` lists it as a chat
   unit with update_pausable=False. hermes-sync `_parkable_gateway_chat` never waits that unit out, and
   `core_update_worker_candidates` refuses it as "unmatched gateway work": the same lease loss as incident 31763,
   with no pause option. The re-queue path (`requeue_unstarted_input`) writes no checkpoint, so it does not need
   a checkpoint-capable scope. Decision: `installed()` now sets ONLY the turn-start gate for a held-class claim
   whose scope is ineligible (no boundary hook, never update_pausable), and removes it after the run.
   `noted_completion` rows are not notifier wakes (their producers are never `kanban-wake`). A managed-worker
   session never binds a scope (`admit_managed` runs before `pause_scope`), so it stays out of scope; this is
   documented on `claim_hold_gate`.

2. Steering transfer while the turn waited on the lease. A queued prompt steered into the claim after its
   turn-start check made `requeue_unstarted_input` refuse ("steering transfer exists"), then the checkpoint
   refused too, and the wake settled interrupted: the wake was lost. `requeue_unstarted` now reads the owner's
   `steer_transfers` after execution returned (the executing stamp is already cleared, so no steer can be
   delivered any more; undelivered tickets are withdrawn from the agent). It passes them to the store, which
   requeues every transfer the turn never took with `_requeue_transfer` in the same write, each in its own FIFO
   place. It still refuses, now with the distinct detail "steering transfer delivered", when a transfer reached
   the turn, and the warning logs that detail. Without `transfers` the old refusal stands.

3. End-to-end test through the real TurnRunner -> AIAgent.run_conversation -> pause_before_turn ->
   project_paused_result -> retain_pause/pending_results -> park_or_interrupt wiring. Only agent allocation
   and the provider are controlled.

Tests (tests/gateway/test_update_hold_claimed_wake.py, tests/hermes_state/test_requeue_unstarted_input.py):
- composed wake re-queue, parametrized [unsupported-scope] (supported() False): RED on origin/main ("the wake
  began its turn inside the update hold"), GREEN here.
- the real `supported` refuses a detached-route kanban wake, which gets only the gate (no hook, not
  pausable): RED (KeyError update_yield_before_turn), GREEN.
- composed steer committed after the turn-start check: RED (wake ('terminal','interrupted')), GREEN (wake
  re-queued, the steered prompt runs once as its own turn, then the wake after the lift, both completed).
- store: an undelivered transfer goes back with the wake in one write (both queued, FIFO order); a delivered
  one refuses "steering transfer delivered"; no transfer read still refuses "steering transfer exists". RED
  (TypeError transfers), GREEN.
- real TurnRunner/AIAgent e2e: no provider request and no transcript row inside the hold, re-queued, then
  one run of the same admission after the lift.
Wider: 28 files importing the changed modules (242 tests) and 23 update-pause/queued-steer files (179) pass;
scripts/check --base origin/main ok.
